1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Baron Ernst George von Brunnow (1796-1845)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was, however, of immense influence on the early propagation of Homoeopathy. In the British journal for April, 1847, is the following :
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernst George von Brunnow, born at Dresden, the 6 th of April, 1796, died there the 5th of May, 1845.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though not a medical man, Von Brunnow has rendered essential services to the cause of Homoeopathy by his literary labors, in connection with the subject.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of noble Courland family, he began in 1829, to devote himself to the study of philosophy and law.
2 CONT
2 CONT His indifferent health prevented him pursuing this path, and he confined himself to the cultivation of the lighter departments of literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT He enjoyed considerable reputation as a novelist, his "Troubadour" and " Ulrick von Hutten," being still popular.
2 CONT
2 CONT Failing to obtain relief from his bodily sufferings from Allopathy, he put himself under Hahnemann's treatment, and obtained such benefit as convinced him of the excellence and truth of the Homoeopathic system, and converted him i
2 CONC nto an ardent champion of the cause.
2 CONT
2 CONT He translated into the French language the " Organon " and several other of Hahnemann's lesser works, and had a considerable share in the Latin translation of the Materia Medica Pura.
2 CONT
2 CONT His last work in connection with Homoeopathy is a small pamphlet entitled, " Ein Blick auf Hahnemann land die Homöopathik." Leipzig, 1844.
2 CONT
2 CONT We cannot better sum up this brief notice of him than in the words of his German biographer -- His whole nature evinced profound feeling, and his melancholy, dark, brilliant eyes betokened clearness of intellect and the noblest of
2 CONC  hearts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Without guile, firm and true in his friendship, sympathizing, unselfish, with an enthusiasm for the beautiful and the sublime, such is our recollection of the noble, the departed Ernst von Brunnow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rapou says that he was a rich inane, with leisure and great talent for writing. He devoted himself entirely to the new doctrines.
2 CONT
2 CONT He prepared an excellent exposition of Homoeopathy for men of the world and translated the " Organon" into French.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stapf thus writes of him :
2 CONT
2 CONT If every one who has labored with active love for the internal and external development of Homoeopathy has a just claim to our grateful recognition, and this, whether he be physician or laymen, then Ernst von Brunnow has quite esp
2 CONC ecially merited to be lovingly mentioned in this journal, and that we should erect a simple memorial to his memory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brunnow was born in Dresden, from a very honorable family of Courland on April 6th, 1796, and died in the same city on the 5th of May, 1815.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been sickly from his earliest child-hood, he was most carefully educated at home until he, in the year 1819, devoted himself in Leipzig to the study of philosophy and jurisprudence, and later on he prepared himself in the B
2 CONC ureau of Justice in Dresden for a higher official position.
2 CONT
2 CONT Increasing bodily ailments, however, soon compelled him to give up this course, and being favored by fortunate external circumstances he lived to himself and his literary undertakings.
2 CONT
2 CONT An honorable testimony as to what he attempted and succeeded in with regard to the field of belles-lettres, where he was quite at home, is afforded to us in a collection of poems, several excellent novels, and especially some very
2 CONC  valuable historical novels, among which the "Troubadour " and " Ulrich von Hutton " deserve especial mention.
2 CONT
2 CONT But he was destined to become useful to science and Humanity also in another manner. The severe bodily sufferings to which he was subjected, and for which he had not even a palliative in Allopathy made him try Homoeopathy, and thi
2 CONC s new method of healing, as administered by the hands of Hahnemann himself, succeeding in essentially improving his condition and in making it at least tolerable.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went through the same experiences as other excellent men in similar circumstances. Overcome by the deep truth of Homoeopathy and by the blessings which it can bestow on mankind when rightly administered he not only became its m
2 CONC ost zealous friend and votary, but he also endeavored after he had most fully became acquainted with its essentials and with its literature, and had made friends with its most distinguished adherents, to contribute with all his st
2 CONC rength by words and deeds to its more general acceptance.
2 CONT
2 CONT For this purpose he translated several works of Hahnemann, the " Organon " and his pamphlet on coffee, etc., into the French language. For this task he was better prepared than many others by his perfect knowledge of that language
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Von Brunnow also took an active part in translating the Materia Medica Pura into Latin. Only those who worked with him and who have carefully watched the progress of Homoeopathy can rightly appreciate how much he has contributed t
2 CONC o it by his excellent translations into French, and by some smaller works about Homoeopathy which he wrote himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also made good use of his manifold social relations in circles which were as distinguished as they were extensive, so as to defend Homoeopathy, which was then so little known and so much misjudged.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the last years of his life, solely in consequence of external influences, he had become favorably disposed to a tendency to Homoeopathy, which in consequence of its peculiar scientific appearance might well appeal to his su
2 CONC sceptible spirit, but which widely differs from true and pure Homoeopathy, as he formerly knew and acknowledged it ; this tendency, however, unless all signs of the times are deceptive, will probably prove but a passing phantom, w
2 CONC hich will soon again give place to the old and eternal truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this spirit he also wrote his last homoeopathic pamphlet : " A Glance at Hahnemann and Homoeopathy, etc." (1844). Part of the contents of this composition we would willingly excuse with Goethe's saying :" Man will err as long a
2 CONC s he strives."
2 CONT
2 CONT In concluding this simple memorial, we would quote the words of another biographer of our beloved friend, because they are as true as they are beautiful. and as suitable as if they had come from my own soul, so that I would not kn
2 CONC ow how to-describe him more suitably :
2 CONT
2 CONT " His whole being showed a warm sentiment from his melancholy dark shining eyes, clearness of spirit, and a most noble heart beamed forth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Without guile, firm and faithful in friendships, sympathetic, self sacrificing, enthusiastic for everything beautiful and great-this is the image of the noble departed Ernst von Brunnow, as it lives in our souls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ave cara anima ! Dr. Stapf.
2 CONT
2 CONT A writer in the Zeitung says :
2 CONT
2 CONT In the night from the 4th to the 5th of May, 1845, died at Dresden, after several weeks of suffering, Baron Ernst Georg - von Brunnow, well-known to the friends of the reformed healing art by his translation into French of Hahnema
2 CONC nn’s Organon, with a very readable preface prefixed to it, entitled Exposé de la Réforme de l' Art Médical. by his French translation of Hahnemann's pamphlet on coffee, and by his cooperation in the Latin translation of the Materi
2 CONC a Medica Pura, undertaken in common with the Drs. Stapf and Gross ; and finally by his work : "A Glance at Hahnemann and Homoeopathy."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born April 6th, 1796, at Dresden, and was the oldest son of a Saxon officer in a high position, who came from Courland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lost his father when very young, but he enjoyed until two years ago, the life and presence of his beloved noble mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the years 1815-19 he studied law at Leipzig, and was afterward for a short time assessor in a Government office at Dresden.
2 CONT
2 CONT But he soon left the civil service owing to his health, and labored for his fellowmen in the advancement of the good and the beautiful through. literary works.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an author, he became known through his "Epos and Lyra," his "Troubadour," his "Ulrich von Hutten," and his "Oberst von Carpezan."
2 CONT
2 CONT His zeal for truth, and his endeavors to advance art and the sciences for the common good of humanity, endeared him to all his friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT For Homoeopathy, he worked with a self-sacrificing love, and he was ever a zealous promoter of the same, though withal with a good common sense.
2 CONT
2 CONT His talents kept equal pace with his zeal ; he was one of those highly gifted authors, who labor and create from an internal impulse.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Brit. Jour. Hom. vol. 5, p. 253. Kleinert, 149, 165. Archiv f. d. hom. Heilk., vol. 22, p1. 2, p. 186. Allg. hom. Zeit., vol. 29, p. 32. Rapou, vol. 2, p. 8h.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
