1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Brännvinsbrännare. James Lyell (known as Jacob Leijel) was born in Arbroath, Scotland on 10/09/1612, the son of Patrick. He arrived in Sweden in 1638 with his brothers Adam, David and Henry. In 1639 on 27 April, Ja
2 CONC mes became a burgess of Stockholm. The previous year, on 21 December, he sought citizenship for his servant James Reiff (Feiff?). It was apparently as a result of his second marriage that James came to own some ironworks. The brot
2 CONC hers became important factory owners in Alvkarled, Rockhammar, Gottbohl (in Ununge Parish) and Harnas. James also had some financial transactions with the Momma-Reenstierna family in Sweden. The brothers also engaged in the Swedis
2 CONC h iron trade, Jacob exporting 55,290 shipounds and David 12,706 shipounds between 1651-60, making them together the third largest exporter of the commodity from Sweden (behind the Momma-Reenstierna brothers and Abraham Bex). They 
2 CONC also co-operated with Adam Raddou, the forth largest exporter who was Jacob Leijel's brother-in-law (adding his exports, the kin group of Leijel and Raddou exported the largest amount of iron, 130,000 ship pounds). The family part
2 CONC ership also saw Raddou and Jacob lease all the Crown's forges and mines in the district of Nora and Linde during the 1660s. In the period between 1661-70, Jacob exported 33,465 ship pounds while David exported 21,980 ship pounds w
2 CONC orth of iron, placing them above the Momma-Reenstierna brothers once more, highlighting their status within the iron export market. By 1674, James served as one of the 48 elders (äldste) of the city of Stockholm. In a letter from 
2 CONC Jacob Fahlgreen to Jacob David on 26 June 1677, Leijel was noted as working with 'Callendar' and employing a factor called John Harwitz. That man shipped 300 pieces of copper money plate to London for Leijel on board the ship of C
2 CONC aptain Heil (Englishman). Jacob got his birthbrieve from Scotland on 15/03/1655. He raised a case against James Feiff on 14 September 1661. In 1688, he received a tax free house "skattehemman" in Knutby parish, Stockholms län. Lei
2 CONC jel married Margaretha Eden (1627-1653) in Stockholm in January 1644 and had two daughters, Gertrude (b.7 May 1645) and Margareta (b. 25 March 1647), a son Jacob (b.19 March 1649), and two further daughters, Eva Maria (b. 12 July 
2 CONC 1650) and Catharina (b.13 August 1651). In 1653 he is listed as one of the 25 shareholders of the Swedish Moscow Company trading in the Baltic to Archangel and Reval. After the death of his first wife he remarried to Barbara Maria
2 CONC  Dress (1632-1694) whose father Andre came from France and mother, Elisabet Schaeij came from the Dutch Republic. James and Barbara had a son, Adam who was ennobled in 1719 and a daughter Maria. After Leijel's death in 1678, he wa
2 CONC s buried in the St Nicholas church in Stockholm around 18 August, his widow Barbera took over the running of the Rockhammar factory. For Raddou to have been Leijel's brother-in-law, he must have married a sister of Jacob, or there
2 CONC  was another wife, or one of the names of the wives is not complete. It should be noted that an important member of the Eastland Company was James sr's brother, Adam, who became a member in 1666 in London. Jakob Patriksson Leijel 
2 CONC was born 10 SEP 1612 in Aberbrothock, Skottland, and died 11 JUL 1678 in Stockholm. He married Margareta Klaesdotter Eden 1 JAN 1644, daughter of Klas Eden Edenberg and Gertrud Sulchen. She was born 18 APR 1627 in Uppsala,C, and d
2 CONC ied 13 DEC 1653 in Nikolai,A. He married Barbara Maria Dress 11 MAR 1655, daughter of Andry Dress and Elisabet Schaeij. She was born 7 JAN 1632 in Gyttorp, Nora,T, and died 14 JUL 1694 in Nikolai,A. In December 1641 Jacob Lyell bo
2 CONC ught in Stockholm several hundred pounds of tobacco which had been landed in Göteborg* from New Sweden, the Swedish colony on the Delaware, by the ship Kalmar Nykel in the spring of 1640. (see "The Swedish Settlements on the Delaw
2 CONC are, 1638-1664" by Dr. Amandus Johnson Vol 1 page 159, published by University of Pennsylvania, 1911.)
2 CONT * Where the cargo was placed in the care of Timon von Schotting, whose grandson Jacob von Schotting married the niece of Jacob Lyell, Magdalena Adamsdotter Leijel, 46 years later!
2 CONT
2 CONT  ........................................................................... Swedish Riksarkiv, Förteckning över Momma-Reenstierna Samlingen, part C: brev till Bröderna Momma-Reenstierna ingångna skrivelser, section 3, Affärer och
2 CONC  processer med nedan nämda enskilda personer, E2595, 127, no date; Swedish Riksarkiv, Bergskollegii skrivelser till Kung. Maj:t. 1640-1840 - Jakob Leijel 1665; Swedish Riksarkiv, Landshövdingarnas Skrivelser till Kung. Maj:t. Upla
2 CONC nds Län - Jacob Leijel, Bruksförvaltare i Knutby sn, Stockholms län, 11/06/1688, 21/10/1690; Stockholm Stadsarkiv: Borgare i Stockholm, Register 1601-1650, p.45; C. Dalhede, Handelsfamiljer pa Stormaktstidens Europamarknad (3 vols
2 CONC , Partille, 2001), II, p.420; Svenska Adelns Ättartavlor, vol. 4, p.521; Steve Murdoch, Network North: Scottish Kin, Commercial and Covert Associations in Northern Europe, 1603-1746 (Brill, Leiden, 2006), pp.181-182; Carl von Linn
2 CONC é 'Nemesis Divina' (1770), p.287. We thank Peter Leyel for this last reference.
